WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.080
"I just, like, you think I could just, like,&nbsp;
borrow that for one second? -Mike, Mike, MIKE!”

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:10.800
This scene from The Bear Season 2&nbsp;
Episode 6 titled "Fishes" is one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.220
of the most suspenseful scenes&nbsp;
I've seen on TV in a long time.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:16.920
[CLIP] "Throw another fork at me,&nbsp;
you're gonna get f***ing rocked!

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.760
-But what makes it so intense?

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:31.560
Is it the fact that the emotional stakes of&nbsp;
the episode were set to simmer 40 minutes ago…

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:33.820
[CLIP] "Carmy, you handle&nbsp;
mom?” "Yeah, I handle mom.”

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:37.020
…Have never had a chance to cool&nbsp;
and are now coming to a head?

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:43.560
Is it the fact that this is the first scene&nbsp;
in the episode where the cacophony of music&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:50.040
and chaotic sounds from the household kitchen&nbsp;
have quieted, leading to an eerie silence?

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:54.840
[CLIP] "I love you, okay? "I love you too, Sugar.”

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:00.000
Is it the electric performances&nbsp;
from the entire cast including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:04.980
Jamie Lee Curtis whose character&nbsp;
has been building in intensity the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:08.400
entire episode And who seems like&nbsp;
they might explode at any moment?

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.540
Those are all key ingredients&nbsp;
but I think what really pulls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:15.960
the scene together and makes the&nbsp;
tension so emotionally harrowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:21.000
and almost overwhelming to watch is&nbsp;
the way the ingredients are assembled.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:25.140
The person responsible for assembling all&nbsp;
these ingredients and serving them to us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:28.740
searching through hours of&nbsp;
footage to choose exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:33.420
which reaction we're going to see at which moment&nbsp;
is Joanna Naugle, one of the editors of The Bear.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:37.680
[Joanna] "There's so many different layers&nbsp;
of like, who is feeling comfortable in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:40.920
situation? Who is feeling stressed?&nbsp;
Who's everyone most focused on?"

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:45.060
-And she expertly draws the viewer&nbsp;
into the emotional intensity of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:48.600
scene by prioritizing a specific kind of shot:

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:51.883
 

00:01:51.883 --> 00:01:56.520
[Joanna] “I think by watching people react to&nbsp;
what is going on, that's actually how you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.880
able to build the tension in the scene, and I&nbsp;
think that's actually how you're able to connect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:04.740
where everyone's head space is, and as much as&nbsp;
possible, I was just trying to clock, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:09.720
Sugar watching Donna, Donna watching Michael,&nbsp;
you know, Cicero watching Carmi watching Michael,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:13.260
like just looking for all the moments of&nbsp;
people clocking each other, and make sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:17.400
that you understood the shared history and&nbsp;
how we got to this point of other chaos.”

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:23.760
These shots, where we're not seeing the character&nbsp;
who's speaking or performing the primary action,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:28.320
but instead the faces of the other&nbsp;
characters reacting, make up almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:32.880
half of the running time of this nearly 14&nbsp;
minute scene. To really underscore just how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:36.780
much narrative work these reaction shots&nbsp;
are doing, consider the fact that Carmy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:42.840
who is one of the shows main characters, doesn’t&nbsp;
speak during this scene. And yet an entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:46.140
emotional arc is convey for his character,&nbsp;
just through the reaction shots we see.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:49.860
To craft a scene like this,&nbsp;
which is made up of 258 edits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:54.960
Joanna has to meticulously comb through&nbsp;
hours of footage to find the perfect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:00.900
expression in a performance that conveys what&nbsp;
a character should be feeling in that moment.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:04.860
But each choice also has to fit within&nbsp;
the larger flow and pace of the scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:09.660
and make sense for the character's&nbsp;
arc in the larger story. And finding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:13.920
the right placement and length of a shot&nbsp;
can come down to a fraction of a second.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:24.600
The reward for this effort is an emotional&nbsp;
pressure cooker. We're not just seeing mounting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:31.560
intensity, we're seeing and feeling all the&nbsp;
emotions around the entire table. The dread,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:37.620
awkward embarrassment, anger, fear, and sadness&nbsp;
about what is playing out in front of them.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:42.240
To edit like this, Joanna has to intimately&nbsp;
understand the story itself and its characters.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:45.360
[Joanna] “Is this whole family this&nbsp;
way? That like if they don't have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:48.840
certain amount of chaos like they literally&nbsp;
will like disappear within themselves and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:52.380
that's why nobody can help themselves at&nbsp;
dinner when it escalates the way it does.”

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:56.880
Character arc, emotional subtext, backstory are&nbsp;
usually things that come up when you're talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:00.780
about writing, acting, and directing.&nbsp;
But when I talk to editors who are at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:04.680
the top of their game, I often notice&nbsp;
how frequently the conversation veers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:08.820
from the technical into the realm&nbsp;
of story, characters, and emotion.

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:09.600
 

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:15.300
The Bear uses editing to its fullest potential.&nbsp;
There are incredible high-intensity chaotic scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:19.440
in the kitchen, beautiful montage sequences,&nbsp;
and many scenes where they understand that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:24.420
the perfect edit is none at all.&nbsp;
But as cool as all this editing is…

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:29.100
[Adam] “It's not style for the sake of&nbsp;
itself, it's style that is serving a purpose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:32.760
either pushing the story ahead or&nbsp;
setting up something thematically.”

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:35.760
…Adam Epstein, another of the&nbsp;
Bears editors, explained how,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:41.640
as flashy as the show's editing gets at times,&nbsp;
it's always grounded in character and story.

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:46.740
"It's less about like, okay, now we're gonna&nbsp;
get crazy more about let's try, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:52.260
as directly as possible to get something into&nbsp;
the interiority of what's going on in Carmen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:56.280
what's going on in Sydney's head,&nbsp;
how Richie is feeling right now.”

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:58.920
I want to know, how exactly is do you do this?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:03.540
How does shot selection and timing&nbsp;
inform a character's emotional arc?

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:08.100
Lets look at how Joanna and Adam used editing to&nbsp;
help construct and motivate Richie's character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:13.080
arc throughout season 2, something they&nbsp;
started setting up in the edit of season 1.

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:17.580
[Joanna] “The first time I think we really&nbsp;
try to create that empathy for him is in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:21.360
second episode where there's that really long&nbsp;
phone call scene. He's sitting with Sydney in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:25.680
the car after being such a jerk to her. And&nbsp;
then all of a sudden he gets a phone call and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:29.520
we just stay on that angle for a really long&nbsp;
time and we just hold on his performance. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:34.320
there's so many places where we could have cut.&nbsp;
And instead Chris and Josh and Joanna were like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:38.280
we should just hang on him this entire time and&nbsp;
seeing him try to be a father. And I think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:41.220
was like the first time we're starting&nbsp;
to feel a little bit of empathy for him.

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:43.920
And in season two, like when he&nbsp;
realizes he was wrong about the mold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:51.060
we just hold on this really long shot of him just&nbsp;
looking really dejected, covered in, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:56.160
stuff that fell on him, leaning into that and&nbsp;
making those feel very long and extended.”

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:00.480
-As an editor, time is one of the most powerful&nbsp;
tools you have at your disposal. So lingering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:06.660
on a piece of performance really can&nbsp;
highlight it for the audience as an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:07.408
important beat. If you changed nothing else&nbsp;
about the show, and just cut these moments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.408 --> 00:06:07.486
where we linger on Richie, we’d feel&nbsp;
entirely different about his character.

00:06:07.486 --> 00:06:10.449
[Joanna] “I think that just emphasizes&nbsp;
like, yes, he's prickly and yes, he's rude,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.449 --> 00:06:15.480
but also like he has a lot of self-loathing. He&nbsp;
has to live with himself also, like that also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:19.740
creates some kind of misery. So that's also how&nbsp;
you can kind of put these breadcrumbs of like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:23.100
I think there's more to him. There's more to him.&nbsp;
He just needs the right environment to flourish.”

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:28.860
If you changed nothing else about the show,&nbsp;
kept all the dialogue the same, but removed all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:34.440
these shots that linger on Richie, we'd feel very&nbsp;
different as an audience about him as a character.

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:36.840
-This gave Adam a foundation to work from when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:39.960
he edited "Forks," Richie's&nbsp;
big episode in season two.

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:44.220
[Adam] “I tried to really lean on the&nbsp;
moments and kind of like the stolen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:48.960
looks in a lot of his closeups, him kind&nbsp;
of internalizing and taking things in.”

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:54.780
- To really sell Richie's arc in "Forks,” one of&nbsp;
the key ingredients was again, reaction shots.

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:59.820
[Adam] “Even if it's a 20 frame shot, but if&nbsp;
that 20 frame shot says "look at me learning"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:06.480
and really taking in what's happening, the&nbsp;
more of that the better. Even if it goes quick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:10.800
you're still getting moments that really&nbsp;
feel like you see something landing in him.”

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:13.980
- But Adam also helped sell&nbsp;
Richie's transformation by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:18.180
expressing what Richie was feeling&nbsp;
internally in the tone of each scene.

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:22.860
[Adam] “We're setting up a tone for something&nbsp;
that's going to evolve over time. If Richie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:27.600
was kind of scared of something, what would&nbsp;
that look like and what would that feel like?”

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:33.780
-For Adam, creating this tone started with&nbsp;
working on the sound design for this scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:36.300
something the editors of The Bear did a lot of.

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:40.860
[Joanna] “I'd say this is probably the show at&nbsp;
least I've done the most temp sound work on.”

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:43.200
[Adam] “Yeah, not even close for me as well.”

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:48.000
[Adam] “When I was building the open to that, it&nbsp;
was more about the underlying kind of sonic bed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:52.380
that would give you this sense of unease that&nbsp;
almost gave it like a bit of a dream logic.

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:56.220
[Clip] “Chef are we done with the forks?”

00:07:56.220 --> 00:08:00.360
[Adam] “The right sound I think lets&nbsp;
you get visually faster a lot of times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:04.440
because you're having two senses that&nbsp;
are clocking something.It lets you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:07.920
kind of be a bit more free-flowing&nbsp;
in kind of the experimentation.”

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:12.180
[A brief aside here, one of the things you'll&nbsp;
notice if you pay close attention watching the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:19.200
bear is that each kitchen environment has its own&nbsp;
unique pace and tone. This is expressed through a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:22.230
combination of set design, cinematography,&nbsp;
sound design, and of course editing.

00:08:22.230 --> 00:08:28.620
[Adam] “When you're in the beef in season one,&nbsp;
it's chaos and it's messy chaos. And then when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:32.040
you're in Empire, which is the high-end&nbsp;
kitchen in episode two of season one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.040 --> 00:08:38.460
it's intense, but it's almost, you know,&nbsp;
like fascistic to an extent. It's really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:43.530
just nailed down and everything is an assembly&nbsp;
line and it's like it's fear-based chaos.”

00:08:43.530 --> 00:08:44.880
]
But back to Forks:

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:49.200
–What's special about this episode is&nbsp;
that the tone of this restaurant changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:52.440
throughout the episode as a reflection&nbsp;
of Richie’s changing perception.

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:55.980
So while tonally we start here…

00:08:55.980 --> 00:08:58.380
…and eventually get to here…

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:02.820
By the end of the episode we end up here…

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:12.900
It's the same location, but to us and&nbsp;
to Richie it feels completely different.

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:18.060
Adam of course used careful editing to show us&nbsp;
Richie's transition between these different tones.

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:24.600
[Adam] “Right after he sees the teachers get&nbsp;
comped the meal and the reaction they have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:30.240
you see you see on his face just him really&nbsp;
taking it in and then the music sort of like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:37.320
it's a gentle rise and then this this crazy dutch&nbsp;
angle of the subway just like it's in slow-mo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:42.180
blasting by that then takes us out to outside&nbsp;
and I kind of saw that as like this is sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:46.200
his mental portal kind of between the two worlds.”

00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:57.060
-When Joanna was working on&nbsp;
the season two episode Sundae,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.060 --> 00:10:01.200
she used a different approach in the&nbsp;
edit to put us inside Sydney’s head.

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:04.440
[Joanna] “We're starting to see her like&nbsp;
piece together things for the new menu.”

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:09.300
-Here it’s an abstract montage that&nbsp;
expresses the character’s internal landscape.

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:12.420
[Joanna] That was something that was really&nbsp;
abstract when it was explained to me.

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:16.620
[Joanna] “I got an initial cut from this other&nbsp;
editor, Naya Amani, who did an amazing job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:20.760
pulling out all these selects. And then I kind&nbsp;
of messed around with a bunch of different music,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:24.240
figured out how to condense it. So it felt&nbsp;
like we were actually going into Sydney's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:28.740
brain and kind of seeing those synapses fire&nbsp;
as she's connecting, you know, seeing snow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:33.780
falling on the river to then seeing Parmesan&nbsp;
falling on pasta, finding match cuts between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:38.580
swatches of colors with windows to then square&nbsp;
ravioli that all kind of had similar shapes.

00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:43.140
And then also putting in photos of like&nbsp;
Ayo actually as a kid, you know, as Sydney,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:47.460
you know, thinking about her past, we put in a&nbsp;
shot of one of the dishes she made in season one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.460 --> 00:10:54.300
trying to visualize what creative inspiration&nbsp;
looks like with match cuts and music and find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.300 --> 00:10:57.480
different connections between the&nbsp;
footage that made it feel abstract,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:02.460
but also relatable, and you could follow that&nbsp;
thread as it's coming to life in her head.

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:06.180
The script for that episode, because they&nbsp;
shot it a little bit more like a documentary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:10.200
they didn't get exactly scripted shots.&nbsp;
We were also kind of working with what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:15.720
was captured And we had a ton, a ton, a ton of&nbsp;
V-roll, Chicago footage, construction footage.

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:19.200
And then on top of that, you know, our&nbsp;
amazing post team was also pulling like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:23.280
oh, can we see, you know, architecture&nbsp;
plans for the corn cob towers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:27.540
like in downtown Chicago? “And they were like,&nbsp;
yep, here's eight options.” And I was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:29.580
this is incredible. This one&nbsp;
matches the angle perfectly.”

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:33.420
[Adam] “The first time I saw what Joanna&nbsp;
put together, I was really blown away.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.420 --> 00:11:36.600
She's selling herself short, I think a&nbsp;
little bit. You know, obviously you need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:41.040
the idea and you need the story, but that&nbsp;
thing was made in her edit in my opinion.”

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:45.540
- This montage itself is beautiful,&nbsp;
but it's also a beautiful example of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:50.400
the show creators are thinking about&nbsp;
editing as a storytelling device.

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:55.680
In this episode, the audience connecting with&nbsp;
Sydney's feeling of inspiration is an important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.680 --> 00:12:01.740
story beat, but instead of relying on dialogue&nbsp;
or exposition to communicate that, the episode's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.740 --> 00:12:08.160
writer and director trusted the editors to be able&nbsp;
to communicate that feeling through the editing.

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:13.140
[Adam] “One of the reasons I think&nbsp;
I've been very lucky to work on this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.140 --> 00:12:18.660
show is the freedom to experiment and&nbsp;
to try interesting things and to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:22.560
what might be a seed of an idea&nbsp;
and really kind of run with it.”

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:26.700
I don't want to rag on the editors of&nbsp;
other movies and shows. Editing is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:32.460
incredibly difficult profession. Bad editing&nbsp;
often isn't even the fault of the editors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.460 --> 00:12:36.840
themselves. But when I watch a lot of shows&nbsp;
and movies though, it really feels like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:42.360
editing is often an afterthought. It's treated&nbsp;
mostly as a way of just assembly what was shot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:46.920
and what was in the script. This is a big part of&nbsp;
editing, but it misses editing's full potential.

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:50.820
Neglecting editing is like putting&nbsp;
all your attention into selecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.820 --> 00:12:54.420
the perfect ingredients, and making sure&nbsp;
they're perfectly cooked. And then just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:57.780
throwing them on a plate without&nbsp;
a care for how they're presented.

00:12:57.780 --> 00:13:03.360
And good editing requires quality&nbsp;
ingredients. The performances, the writing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:09.360
and the cinematography -all have to be doing&nbsp;
their job in order for good editing to take place.

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:13.680
This freedom that The Bear’s editors had really&nbsp;
helped establish editing as part of the language&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:19.140
of the show, when get the audience used to editing&nbsp;
being a part of the expression of the character’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.140 --> 00:13:25.440
internal space, you can more freely do things&nbsp;
in the edit that might feel jarring otherwise.

00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:30.180
For example when Carmy ends up locked in the&nbsp;
freezer in Season 2, Joanna was able to start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:37.140
creating a montage of shots from other episodes&nbsp;
that express what Carmy is feeling and imagining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.140 --> 00:13:42.120
in that moment- and it doesn’t feel like a&nbsp;
flashback, instead it feels seamless because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.120 --> 00:13:47.580
the show has time and time again used insert&nbsp;
shots in this kind of expressionistic way.

00:13:47.580 --> 00:13:50.160
And when you trust your editors in this way and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:55.620
when those editors really understand&nbsp;
the story and character motivations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.620 --> 00:14:02.340
there's potential for the script to be&nbsp;
reshaped in ways that benefit the story:

00:14:02.340 --> 00:14:05.520
[Adam] “In season one, episode&nbsp;
two, kind of when they're talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.520 --> 00:14:07.920
about the health inspector potentially coming.”

00:14:07.920 --> 00:14:09.780
[Clip] “She's not nice, she's not your friend.”

00:14:09.780 --> 00:14:13.440
[Adam] “...and Tina and Ibrahim&nbsp;
are outside talking to each other.”

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:15.420
[Clip] “Look around, this ain't no more.”

00:14:15.420 --> 00:14:19.200
[Adam] “...and then Karmi and Sid are&nbsp;
talking about what needs to happen.”

00:14:19.200 --> 00:14:21.420
[Clip] “So we could just have&nbsp;
somebody go and pick it up.”

00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:25.800
[Adam] “All these are scripted as like,&nbsp;
here's a scene, here's a scene, here's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:29.400
a scene. They're not scripted as far as cross&nbsp;
cutting. This is more something that kind of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:34.680
I think Joanna really did such a great job of&nbsp;
having a track that is playing kind of throughout,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:39.780
and yet you're dipping back into these&nbsp;
conversations, but it doesn't feel as manic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.780 --> 00:14:43.260
as like, you know, a full cross cutting&nbsp;
thing. And it doesn't feel as montage-y&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.260 --> 00:14:47.700
as a full montage-y, but it's definitely&nbsp;
not just, you know, divided scene work.”

00:14:47.700 --> 00:14:51.300
[Joanna] “Yeah, we did that&nbsp;
again in episode 208 also.

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:56.400
There was a scene where Carmi and Fack are in the&nbsp;
fridge and they're talking about Claire and like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.400 --> 00:15:00.180
they're like, what is a girlfriend? And that&nbsp;
was filmed as a one take, but it was kind of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.180 --> 00:15:04.020
long scene and it actually felt like an instance&nbsp;
where the one take probably wasn't gonna work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.020 --> 00:15:09.120
So that was something that Megan and I, who&nbsp;
edited the episode with me, talked about what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.120 --> 00:15:12.720
else we could like interweave. And we almost&nbsp;
like braided together three or four scenes.

00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:17.040
So we weren't only in this one take, we were&nbsp;
kind of coming back to it as like the tent poles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:21.300
but we had this one beautiful December song&nbsp;
underneath it. And then it was like, you know, a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:25.200
little bit of Fack and Carmi in the fridge.&nbsp;
Then we go to Tina and Richie, then we're back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:29.460
to Fack and Carmi, then we're with Richie and&nbsp;
Sydney. And it was just a way to kind of be in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.460 --> 00:15:33.646
multiple places at once, keep the pace going,&nbsp;
but also link them all at the same time too.”

00:15:33.646 --> 00:15:36.840
[Adam] “And that type of thing is a great example&nbsp;
of, you know, when people talk about like editing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.840 --> 00:15:41.340
being the final rewrite. It's not scripted. All&nbsp;
the words that are said might be there, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.340 --> 00:15:45.300
order and the way that the scenes are weaving in&nbsp;
and out of each other, that's not on the page.

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:49.380
That's something that you gotta experiment&nbsp;
and you figure it out and you find how do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.380 --> 00:15:52.920
you get the truest version of what's&nbsp;
written but in a way that allows the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:58.260
tools of filmmaking to elevate it to&nbsp;
what its most ideal form could be.”

00:15:58.260 --> 00:16:03.060
-Editing when used to it’s full potential, isn’t&nbsp;
just the means by which scenes are assembled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.060 --> 00:16:08.580
or the best performances are chosen- it is&nbsp;
itself a big part of the language of filmic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.580 --> 00:16:13.020
storytelling. The Bear showcases what’s possible&nbsp;
when everyone from the writers to the directors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.020 --> 00:16:18.240
to the editors themselves really understand&nbsp;
the motivation and emotions behind the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:23.209
and it’s characters- and how editing can bring&nbsp;
those characters and their experiences to life.

00:16:23.209 --> 00:16:23.220
 

00:16:23.220 --> 00:16:26.820
One of the best examples I’ve seen&nbsp;
in a movie recently of innovative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.820 --> 00:16:32.700
editing that draws you into the story,&nbsp;
is Park Chan-Wook’s Decision To Leave.

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:39.420
This is an example of a director&nbsp;
who meticulously planned the edit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.420 --> 00:16:44.580
before they even started shooting&nbsp;
as you can see in these storyboards.

